{
  "generated_text": "To convert the point \\((-4, 0)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we need to find the values of \\(r\\) and \\(\\theta\\). The formulas for the conversion are:\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\]\n\\[ \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\]\n\nGiven the point \\((-4, 0)\\), we have \\(x = -4\\) and \\(y = 0\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(r\\):\n\n\\[ r = \\sqrt{(-4)^2 + 0^2} = \\sqrt{16} = 4 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\theta\\). The formula for \\(\\theta\\) is:\n\n\\[ \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n\\[ \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{0}{-4}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}(0) \\]\n\nThe value of \\(\\tan^{-1}(0)\\) is \\(0\\) or \\(\\pi\\) (since the tangent function is periodic with period \\(\\pi\\)). However, we need to consider the quadrant in which the point \\((-4, 0)\\) lies. The point \\((-4, 0)\\) is on the negative \\(x\\)-axis, which corresponds to an angle of \\(\\pi\\) radians.\n\nThus, \\(\\theta = \\pi\\).\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates are:\n\n\\[ (r, \\theta) = \\left(4, \\pi\\right) \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(4, \\pi)}\n\\]",
